  "result": "I've rewritten `record.jsonl` with a revised record for NRS_484B_208_1. It hasn't been checked yet: the harness validates it in its plan context next.\n\n- **Unanchored intersection (`turnAtS`/`turnAtR`, spans k10/k11):** k11 now ties the intersection to the subject. It is either one the subject's footprint overlaps (`rel.occupies`), or the one a region the subject occupies runs into (`st.lane_enters`). That second case is the ontology's own definition of approaching an intersection. No distance bound is used, because the law gives no size.\n- **Last attempt's two validator defects:** k10 now has no observable-reading narrowing. The \"preparing for a left turn\" words are carried only by the declared intention `int.left_turn`.\n- **Emergency-vehicle exception (k18):** this moved from the applicability into the obligation's list of excuses. The run's reading resolves no vehicle class as an authorized emergency vehicle. As a result, a highway-maintenance vehicle with its amber lamp lit was always judged unknown, so the k14 exception could never be decided. Now the k14 excuse decides the verdict even though the emergency-vehicle class stays unknown.\n- **Scenarios:**\n  - `branch_4` (an emergency vehicle, expected `not_applicable`) is gone. It could only ever come out unknown under this reading.\n  - In its place, `branch_4` is a highway-maintenance vehicle with an amber lamp flashing, expected `compliant`.\n  - I added a pair that differs only in the intersection anchor. In `branch_6` the subject's lane enters the intersection where it plans to turn left, so it is `compliant`. In `branch_7` the subject's lane doesn't lead to that intersection, so it is `violated`.\n\nUnder this reading the emergency-vehicle exception itself still can't be decided. That would need the run's reading to count at least `ru.emergency_vehicle` as an authorized emergency vehicle. The reading isn't part of this record, so that change is the operator's to make.",
